 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Ten days ago Zane Li was in Paris accepting the Karl Lagerfeld Award from the LVMH Prize for a collection he created in three weeks to present to the judges. (“I was so tired of all the past-season stuff,” the designer said.) Li’s ability to craft such an electric lineup so quickly seems to confirm what many suspect: that the 25-year-old is something of a prodigy. Certainly he’s influential; his flat construction, eye for color, and interest in melding sophistication and sportiness have clearly inspired others.</p><p>Among the things that influenced Li this season were Jeanne Lanvin’s archive and Alex Katz’s paintings; see the kid-like hood accessories and the perfect LBDs. A more abstract reference was the song “Emotions,” recorded in 1960 by Brenda Lee when she was just 15 and released the following year. “It’s about emotions for love, for life,” Li said. “I was really surprised that someone that age could actually express those feelings so well, so I was wondering, do I have to live to feel, or are the emotions we feel at the moment real to us?”</p><p>This led the designer to think about the relationship between girlhood and womanhood and, more fundamentally, his relationship to ready-to-wear. “It’s always a tension and a battle to me as a 25-year-old Chinese person trying to explore what a woman would really like, and I think that tension is quite interesting in a way,” he said. “Maybe some things come out that might not be functional or might not be traditional womenswear in a lot of people’s eyes, but I enjoy that offness.” Li is turning others on to it, too.</p><p>The spring collection featured many pieces that were actually skewed, most dramatically in tank tops that weren’t centered and interacted with miniskirts like children’s Colorforms shapes. Asymmetries included a patent flange on one side of a sleeveless dress; a giant fall on one side of a two-tone dress cut from scuba material; and single-sock styling. More fundamentally, the way Li arrives at dimensionality through flat patternmaking is most disorienting and inspiring.</p><p>Given this season’s interest in youth and youthquake fashions (somewhere André Courrèges and Pierre Cardin are smiling), you could say the collection had a slight paper-doll quality. This is especially apt given the concurrent run of Tom Sachs’s “<a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/search/31+rue+Cambon?entry=gmail&amp;source=g\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">31 rue Cambon</a>” installation featuring plywood fashions and the “Doll Dressing” exhibition soon to open at the Museum at FIT. All of these bodies of work might subconsciously speak, to some degree, to the leveling of culture through screens in a digital age.</p><p>Li’s skill is to “carbonate” fashion by making the contrast between the body, movement, and flatness pop. He achieves that through color and simple shapes that suggest movement, energy, a girl on the go. She’s mod, with a bod, and dangerous to know—in other words, irresistible.</p></div>",
